---
title: Chat
name: Chat
description: Post to Slack, Discord, Teams or Telegram with chat() — the same shape as mail() and sms().
category: Channels
---

```typescript
import { chat } from "postboi"

await chat({ title: "Deploy", message: "Finished in 42s" })
```

The thinnest channel postboi has: no addresses to parse, no encoding to worry about, no
delivery receipts. For Slack, Discord and Teams the destination lives inside the webhook
URL, so there's usually nothing to pass but the message.

## Setup

Three of the four need only a URL. **Treat it as a secret** — anyone holding it can post to
your channel.

```bash
# .env
POSTBOI_CHAT_PROVIDER=slack
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/…
```

| Provider | Import | Credential |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Slack | `postboi/slack` | `SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL` |
| Discord | `postboi/discord` | `DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL` |
| Microsoft Teams | `postboi/teams` | `TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL` |
| Telegram | `postboi/telegram` | `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` + a `chat_id` |

## Microsoft Teams needs a Workflows URL

⚠️ **Office 365 connectors were disabled in May 2026.** If you have an old
`…office.com/webhook/…` URL, it no longer delivers — silently.

The replacement is a **Power Automate Workflows** webhook: in Teams, pick a channel →
**Workflows** → *Post to a channel when a webhook request is received*. Its URL lives on
`logic.azure.com`. We post an [Adaptive Card](https://adaptivecards.io), which is
Microsoft's current guidance — Workflows still accepts the legacy MessageCard shape for
migration, but it drops interactive elements.

## Telegram is a registered identity, not an address

A bot **cannot message someone who hasn't started a chat with it first**, and you address
people by `chat_id` — a number you capture from an inbound update, not something you can
know in advance.

```typescript
import Telegram from "postboi/telegram"

const bot = new Telegram({ bot_token: process.env.TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN })
await bot.send({ to: "987654321", message: "Deploy finished" })
```

That's the same shape as a push subscription token, so it needs somewhere to be stored.
Slack, Discord and Teams have no equivalent problem — the URL *is* the destination.

## Titles

`title` renders wherever the platform has somewhere to put one — bold mrkdwn on Slack, bold
markdown on Discord, a heading text block on Teams:

```typescript
await chat({ title: "Build failed", message: "3 tests failing on main" })
```

## Development posts for real

Unlike [SMS](/sms), chat is **not** intercepted in development. Posting to your own Slack
channel while developing is normally the point: it costs nothing, reaches only your team,
and you can delete it. The SMS interception exists because a stray text costs money and
can't be recalled — neither applies here.

With no provider configured at all, messages are logged instead.

## Posting to more than one place

`to` overrides the configured destination per message, so one provider can serve several
channels:

```typescript
await chat([
	{ to: process.env.SLACK_ALERTS, message: "🔴 Down" },
	{ to: process.env.SLACK_DEPLOYS, message: "Deployed abc123" },
])
```

Each gets its own result — one failure never loses the rest.

## Limits worth knowing

- **Discord** caps a message at 2000 characters. We truncate rather than let the whole post
  be rejected, which matters when you're piping in a stack trace.
- **Slack** replies with a plain-text reason on failure (`no_service`, `invalid_payload`)
  rather than JSON, so that's what lands in `error.code`.
- **Telegram** reports failures with `ok: false` and an **HTTP 200**, so don't check status
  yourself — the normalized error already accounts for it.
